# A viscoelastic block that hardly bounces, under light rain
law mochi_in_the_rain
kind world
claim a viscoelastic block that hardly bounces, under light rain
param raining switch = on
param strength number = 4 range 0 100
material mochi density = 1100
material mochi friction = 0.6
material mochi bounce = 0.2
source shear modulus: Isono, Okamura, Fujimoto and Watanabe, Agric. Biol. Chem. 54(11):2941-2947 (1990), doi:10.1271/bbb1961.54.2941, Fig. 4
source the storage modulus G' at 30 C and 0.1 Hz of mochi kneaded 10 min, water content 45.5 %, frozen and then kept 48 h at 2-4 C before the test
source digitized from a log plot: log10 G' (dyn/cm^2) = 6.63 +- 0.03, so G' = 4.27e5 Pa within -7 % / +7 %
source the condition is 30 C: room temperature (20-25 C) is not covered and not extrapolated; the modulus falls steeply above 40 C (Fig. 6)
source Poisson's ratio 0.49 is assumed for a water-rich gel and was not measured
material mochi shear_modulus = 427000
material mochi shear_modulus basis measured
material mochi poisson_ratio = 0.49
material mochi poisson_ratio basis assumed
source loss modulus: same figure (Fig. 4, lower panel), same sample and conditions (30 C, 0.1 Hz): log10 G'' (dyn/cm^2) = 4.98, 5.01, 5.02 at three strains, so G'' = 5.00 +- 0.02, 1.00e4 Pa within -5 % / +5 %, loss tangent 0.023
source other frequencies at 30 C are not readable from the paper (Fig. 7 is a perspective mesh; Figs. 9 and 10 are at 90 C after gelatinization), so no relaxation model is fitted and only the 0.1 Hz pair is stated
material mochi loss_modulus = 10000
material mochi loss_modulus basis measured
material mochi modulus_frequency = 0.1
source adhesion: no measured force at which a hand's hold on fresh mochi lets go was found; 2 N is assumed as a tack stress of about 10 kPa over the contact area below
source contact area: a finger pad pressed on a soft surface, about 2 cm^2, assumed
material mochi adhesion = 2
material mochi adhesion basis assumed
material mochi contact_area = 0.0002
material mochi contact_area basis assumed
source hand: finger skin Young's modulus about 310 kPa by cylindrical indentation with a Hertzian model ("Insights into the Young's modulus of human finger skin: evaluating contact area and indentation creep behaviour", Acta Technica Napocensis, atna-mam.utcluj.ro article 2661); reported values span about 40 to 300 kPa by method, so it is used as assumed
source hand Poisson's ratio 0.48 is assumed for a water-rich soft tissue and was not measured
source grip: the stiffness is derived (flat contact on a half-space, the mochi and the skin in series); the contact radius about 8 mm is small next to a letter 160 mm deep, so the half-space holds
hand_youngs_modulus = 310000
hand_youngs_modulus basis assumed
hand_youngs_modulus spread 40000 310000
hand_poisson_ratio = 0.48
hand_poisson_ratio basis assumed
rain = raining
rain_rate = strength
